1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,646
This red stripe across the Pacific
wasn't there a year ago.

2
00:00:04,156 --> 00:00:07,059
NASA satellites
have watched it grow all year.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:08,870
It's El Niño.

4
00:00:09,195 --> 00:00:12,725
And it has already reached one
of the driest deserts on Earth.

5
00:00:13,537 --> 00:00:18,796
In August, snow buried the plateau
where a giant radio telescope stands.

6
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:21,339
Then the desert started to bloom.

7
00:00:21,664 --> 00:00:23,359
Here's what the satellites saw.

8
00:00:23,684 --> 00:00:26,436
These maps show the surface of the ocean.

9
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:30,557
Red means water warmer
than normal for that time of year.

10
00:00:30,801 --> 00:00:32,450
Blue means cooler.

11
00:00:32,833 --> 00:00:36,966
Normally, steady trade winds
push warm surface water west,

12
00:00:37,012 --> 00:00:38,870
toward Asia and Australia.

13
00:00:39,195 --> 00:00:44,222
When those winds weaken, that warm water
slides back east, toward South America.

14
00:00:44,826 --> 00:00:49,853
That long warm band along the equator
is the signature of El Niño.

15
00:00:50,364 --> 00:00:54,648
According to NASA, El Niño
officially arrived in June 2026.

16
00:00:54,892 --> 00:00:58,038
And NOAA's forecasters expected
it to keep strengthening

17
00:00:58,096 --> 00:00:59,582
through the end of the year.

18
00:00:59,907 --> 00:01:01,951
Satellites don't just measure temperature.

19
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:06,792
This map compares the height
of the sea surface, 2025 against 2026.

20
00:01:07,117 --> 00:01:08,789
Warm water expands.

21
00:01:09,032 --> 00:01:12,713
So where the ocean heats up,
its surface stands a little higher,

22
00:01:12,806 --> 00:01:15,766
and radar satellites can measure
that bump from orbit.

23
00:01:16,045 --> 00:01:17,020
Then there's color.

24
00:01:17,403 --> 00:01:21,165
NASA's PACE satellite
launched in February 2024.

25
00:01:21,490 --> 00:01:26,343
This is the first complete El Niño it gets
to watch, with near-daily measurements

26
00:01:26,378 --> 00:01:30,116
across far more shades of color
than an ordinary camera can see.

27
00:01:30,627 --> 00:01:32,484
Green means phytoplankton,

28
00:01:32,728 --> 00:01:36,060
the microscopic plants at the base
of the ocean food chain.

29
00:01:36,443 --> 00:01:39,845
Compare June 2025 with June 2026.

30
00:01:40,019 --> 00:01:44,593
In the central equatorial Pacific,
that green band thinned out.

31
00:01:44,872 --> 00:01:47,693
Less plankton means less food
for everything above it.

32
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:49,481
Fish. Seabirds.

33
00:01:49,597 --> 00:01:50,700
Marine mammals.

34
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:54,706
This year Peru repeatedly
suspended its anchovy fishery,

35
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:59,071
and pelicans turned up in Peruvian
ports and cities looking for food.

36
00:01:59,396 --> 00:02:02,821
Now follow that warm water
east, to the coast of Chile.

37
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:08,058
Starting in mid-July, storm fronts fed
by an atmospheric river brought one

38
00:02:08,093 --> 00:02:10,763
of the heaviest July
rain events in decades,

39
00:02:10,972 --> 00:02:13,503
according to Chile's
national weather service.

40
00:02:14,107 --> 00:02:18,228
Then, in August, back-to-back
winter storms brought snow.

41
00:02:18,669 --> 00:02:21,827
This is the Chajnantor
plateau on August 6th.

42
00:02:22,268 --> 00:02:25,276
And this is the same
plateau on August 14th.

43
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:29,618
It's home to ALMA, a giant
array of radio antennas.

44
00:02:30,059 --> 00:02:32,277
As the snow and high winds moved in,

45
00:02:32,486 --> 00:02:37,989
ALMA suspended operations and turned
its antennas to a protective survival mode.

46
00:02:37,989 --> 00:02:42,203
In the coastal town of Taltal,
nearly 40 millimeters of rain,

47
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,535
about an inch and a half,
fell in three days.

48
00:02:45,977 --> 00:02:49,309
That's about ten times
its normal amount for a whole year.

49
00:02:49,912 --> 00:02:54,997
One Chilean atmospheric scientist said
events like this happen only a few times,

50
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:56,715
if any, per decade.

51
00:02:57,319 --> 00:03:00,152
Water in a desert doesn't
stay hidden for long.

52
00:03:00,593 --> 00:03:04,018
Seeds that had waited
in the dry ground began to sprout.

53
00:03:04,715 --> 00:03:08,476
Look at these hills southwest
of the city of Copiapó.

54
00:03:08,987 --> 00:03:10,264
September 14th.

55
00:03:10,624 --> 00:03:12,319
Then October 24th.

56
00:03:12,969 --> 00:03:16,754
That reddish-purple tint
is a flower called pata de guanaco.

57
00:03:17,172 --> 00:03:19,784
Scientists call this a flowering desert.

58
00:03:20,098 --> 00:03:23,256
A 2019 study counted
thirteen of these blooms

59
00:03:23,314 --> 00:03:26,169
between 1981 and 2015.

60
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:31,324
But one researcher says this one appears
to be the most significant yet, in extent,

61
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:36,676
intensity and duration, at least since
scientists began watching from space.

62
00:03:37,373 --> 00:03:38,708
NASA is careful here:

63
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:43,607
these rains came as El Niño
was strengthening in the tropical Pacific.

64
00:03:44,048 --> 00:03:47,311
The two arrived together,
and the satellites caught both.

65
00:03:47,752 --> 00:03:49,110
So what happens now?

66
00:03:50,062 --> 00:03:55,333
As of August, NOAA gave a ninety-seven
percent chance that this El Niño lasts

67
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,829
into early spring of 2027.

68
00:03:58,433 --> 00:04:00,499
And history suggests a twist.

69
00:04:00,882 --> 00:04:03,170
La Niña often follows El Niño.

70
00:04:03,344 --> 00:04:07,605
After the strong La Niña of 1998 and 1999,

71
00:04:07,779 --> 00:04:11,993
a huge phytoplankton bloom spread
across the eastern Pacific.

72
00:04:12,376 --> 00:04:16,509
If that happens again, the next big
change won't show up first in the weather.

73
00:04:16,625 --> 00:04:18,936
It will show up in the color of the ocean.

74
00:04:19,063 --> 00:04:21,362
And NASA's satellites will be watching.
